Falco runs as a service on the worker node `{{NODE01}}` and reads `/etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml`. You need to detect any container that opens the sensitive device `/dev/mem`.

On `{{NODE01}}` add a rule named **`Detect dev mem access`** to `/etc/falco/falco_rules.local.yaml`:

- the condition checks that `fd.name` is exactly `/dev/mem` (other files, for example `/dev/mem2`, must not trigger it);
- the output is exactly: `Sensitive file opened (user=%user.name command=%proc.cmdline file=%fd.name)`;
- the priority is `WARNING`.

Make sure the running Falco loads the rule, then trigger it by creating a Pod in the namespace `memory-watch` on `{{NODE01}}` that opens `/dev/mem`. The validation also runs its own harmless probe Pod there.
